{"id":"kor-t0-0001","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.hello","domain":1,"text_native":"안녕하십니까.","text_romanized":"annyeonghasimnikka","text_english":"Hello.","source_caption":"Hello.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0002","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.morning","domain":1,"text_native":"좋은 아침입니다.","text_romanized":"jo-eun achimimnida","text_english":"Good morning.","source_caption":"Good morning.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used until roughly midday. Getting the time of day wrong is noticed; not saying it is only noticed by someone expecting it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0003","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.afternoon","domain":1,"text_native":"오후","text_romanized":"ohu","text_english":"Good afternoon.","source_caption":"afternoon","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The middle of the day. In several languages this is the same word as the evening greeting, and using it late is not an error.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0004","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.evening","domain":1,"text_native":"좋은 저녁입니다.","text_romanized":"jo-eun jeonyeogimnida","text_english":"Good evening.","source_caption":"Good evening.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"From late afternoon. Often interchangeable with the afternoon greeting.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0005","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.how-are-you","domain":1,"text_native":"어떻게 지내십니까?","text_romanized":"eotteoke jinaesimnikka?","text_english":"How are you?","source_caption":"How are you?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The opening move in almost every conversation. In most of these languages it is a greeting rather than a question and does not expect a literal answer.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0006","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.i-am-well","domain":1,"text_native":"잘 지냅니다, 감사합니다.","text_romanized":"jal jinaemnida, gamsahamnida","text_english":"I am well, thank you.","source_caption":"Fine, thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The expected answer to the greeting above. Answering it wrong reads as evasive in cultures where it is a formula.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0007","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.nice-to-meet-you","domain":1,"text_native":"만나서 반갑습니다.","text_romanized":"mannaseo ban-gapseumnida","text_english":"Nice to meet you.","source_caption":"Nice to meet you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said on a first introduction. In several languages there is a separate phrase for meeting someone formally that a stranger will use instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0008","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"greeting.what-is-your-name","domain":1,"text_native":"성함이 어떻게 되세요?","text_romanized":"seonghami eotteoke doeseyo?","text_english":"What is your name?","source_caption":"What is your name?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"You get asked this before you get to ask it. Recognising the question is the difference between answering and freezing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0009","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.please","domain":1,"text_native":"부탁합니다.","text_romanized":"butakamnida","text_english":"Please.","source_caption":"Please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Not a filler in English, and a grammatical particle in some of these languages — one word that changes a sentence from an order to a request.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0010","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.thank-you","domain":1,"text_native":"감사합니다.","text_romanized":"gamsahamnida","text_english":"Thank you.","source_caption":"Thank you.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The single most useful word in any of these languages. Saying it costs nothing and changes how everything after it is received.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0011","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.youre-welcome","domain":1,"text_native":"천만입니다.","text_romanized":"cheonmanimnida","text_english":"You are welcome.","source_caption":"You're welcome.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"You will be handed it constantly, and there is no way to avoid replying. In a few languages the reply is the same word as 'yes'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0012","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.excuse-me","domain":1,"text_native":"실례합니다.","text_romanized":"sill(y)e hamnida","text_english":"Excuse me.","source_caption":"Excuse me. (getting attention)","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Two different things in one phrase: getting someone's attention, and getting past them. Guessing wrong is jarring — one version walks through a conversation.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0013","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.sorry","domain":1,"text_native":"죄송합니다. or 미안해 (mianhae)","text_romanized":"joesonghamnida","text_english":"I am sorry.","source_caption":"I'm sorry.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Apologising for a small fault — bumping someone, being late, not understanding. The form you use signals how much of a fault you think it was.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0014","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"courtesy.goodbye","domain":1,"text_native":"안녕히 가십시오/계십시오.","text_romanized":"annyeonghi gasipsio/gyesipsio","text_english":"Goodbye.","source_caption":"Goodbye","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"How you leave. Leaving without one is noticeably rude in most of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0015","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"repair.dont-understand","domain":12,"text_native":"이해가 안 갑니다.","text_romanized":"ihaega an gamnida","text_english":"I do not understand.","source_caption":"I don't understand.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The one phrase that keeps a conversation alive. Without it people stop trying, and with it they often rephrase in simpler words.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0016","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"repair.speak-slower","domain":12,"text_native":"천천히 말해 주십시오.","text_romanized":"cheoncheonhi malhae jusipsio","text_english":"Please speak more slowly.","source_caption":"Please speak slowly.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Asks for the thing you actually need. Saying it is nearly always received well, even badly pronounced, and asking again is not.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t0-0017","lang":"kor","tier":0,"slot":"bridge.little-english","domain":12,"text_native":"네, 조금만요.","text_romanized":"ne, jogeummanyo","text_english":"A little English.","source_caption":"Yes, a little.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The answer to the question above, and the thing that tells a speaker to switch to simpler words rather than to English.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
